erightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx2026-07-20T13:30:44.613Zƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;9MsM6TdxK1DacDim8kuyUe3Q26AGYdPaunkA-3kYCEwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxihttps://ictnews.org/news/deaf-and-native-hawaiian-%ca%bbolelo-sign-language-offers-a-cultural-connection/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;QyRo_7YsnuI0RgdlLUph2-FHo72_0ETNiYBzzGxMLOkƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9https://newscable-source-broker.invalid/activitypub/actorƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;i1V0W2ue-DYLOhxYAC-Z07mM4XCD1OE8ZTIWOQH0LH0ƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;iaQY_q2NSnVWzOeE0nOHK9iz4z18fqg7VZYwUsyNbBQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxihttps://ictnews.org/news/deaf-and-native-hawaiian-%ca%bbolelo-sign-language-offers-a-cultural-connection/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;8VHke3vkF3R5rVf_NQSvBrqXocGgUCMmoXR-9yR_ew4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky#ıThis story was produced by Honolulu Civil Beat , a nonprofit news organization covering HawaiÊ»i that specializes in accountability and in-depth enterprise coverage. For more stories like this, subscribe to their newsletters . Tia Lewis Honolulu Civil Beat Growing up on OÊ»ahu &nbsp; in the 1950s, Maile Keamoai-Kane learned a lot from her father: how to prepare laulau, gather Ê»opihi, and catch and clean fish. He never excluded her.&nbsp; But being deaf, Keamoai-Kane struggled to learn another key aspect of Hawaiian culture: the language. Her father did the best he could teaching her improvised gestures with his hands to teach her some words, but there was no established sign language based in Ê»ÅŒlelo HawaiÊ»i, the Hawaiian language. Instead, as a student at the HawaiÊ»i School for the Deaf and the Blind, Keamoai-Kane said she was forced to read lips. Later in high school, she learned American Sign Language, or ASL, which uses signs based in American and French culture. â€œI wanted to learn Ê»ÅŒlelo HawaiÊ»i,â€ she said. â€œBut at the time, the deaf school did not have a program. That is one of my regrets.â€ Today, there is a small but growing effort to establish hand signs rooted in Hawaiian words and cultural values. Called Ê»ÅŒlelo HawaiÊ»i Kuhi Lima â€” Hawaiian Language Hand Signs â€” the community-led initiative is working to create signs that preserve and honor Ê»ÅŒlelo and make the islandsâ€™ Native language more accessible to the Deaf community.&nbsp;&nbsp; â€œWe want to ensure that Deaf KÄnaka have a meaningful place within the greater lÄhui (collective identity),â€ said Keamoai-Kane, whose married name is Paongo.&nbsp; The effort was started in 2024 by University of HawaiÊ»i student Kekai Kaaumoana-Cummings, who is majoring in Ê»ÅŒlelo HawaiÊ»i and is the first deaf person at UH MÄnoa in Kawaihuelani Ka HÄlau Ê»ÅŒlelo HawaiÊ»i â€” the Department of Hawaiian Language.&nbsp; Kaaumoana-Cummings hopes to become a teacher and bridge the gap so Deaf Native Hawaiians have greater access to learning their Indigenous language.&nbsp; â€œThe project aims to develop new programs for Hawaiian immersion education that are academically accessible and community-based, providing opportunities for future growth,â€ he wrote in messages with Civil Beat. The group has already created and discussed dozens of signs, but the system is still unofficial and developing. Some signs focus on everyday vocabulary, Hawaiian cultural concepts and Ê»ÅŒlelo HawaiÊ»i grammar structures.&nbsp; Kaaumoana-Cummings shared that they are still organizing and developing the signs carefully before publicly releasing an official list. However, simple words such as&nbsp; no, yes, house, land &nbsp;and&nbsp; mountain &nbsp;have been established.&nbsp; In ASL, â€œhouseâ€ is signed as though you were to outline the shape of a house with both hands, starting from the tip of the roof down to the sides of the walls. In Ê»ÅŒlelo HawaiÊ»i Kuhi Lima, â€œhaleâ€ is signed with fingertips touching to form the shape of a roof, tapping them together.&nbsp; â€œÊ»ÅŒlelo HawaiÊ»i Kuhi Lima continues to evolve,â€ Kaaumoana-Cummings wrote, â€œlaying a foundation for future generations to learn, use, and expand our language visually.â€&nbsp; Sign Languages In HawaiÊ»i Similar to spoken languages, sign languages vary by country, according to Keane Nakapueo-Garcia, a hearing PhD student at UH Hilo who is currently doing his own research into the signs historically used by Deaf Native Hawaiians to communicate.&nbsp; For example, American, British, Australian and New Zealand sign languages are all distinct despite those countries sharing English as a spoken language. And American Sign Language (ASL) is not as simple as English communicated through hand movements. It is an independent language with its own culture â€” Deaf culture.&nbsp; There is evidence that hand signs were used in HawaiÊ»i prior to the introduction of ASL in the islands in the 1940s, and University of HawaiÊ»i researchers in 2013 declared the existence of a HawaiÊ»i Sign Language. But those signs donâ€™t have a foundation in Ê»ÅŒlelo HawaiÊ»i or Native Hawaiian culture, according to Kaaumoana-Cummings and Nakapueo-Garcia. HSL is based on different cultures and spoken languages that were prevalent during the plantation-era â€” including Chinese, Japanese and Filipino â€” and is more akin to Pidgin. For example, in HSL the sign for â€œyellowâ€ is related to cutting pineapple, a reference to plantation work that has no relation to Ê»ÅŒlelo HawaiÊ»i.&nbsp; The work of Ê»ÅŒlelo HawaiÊ»i Kuhi Lima is about protecting Native Hawaiian history and identity, Keamoai-Kane wrote in messages with Civil Beat. â€œOur group came together because we saw an urgent need to give a clear and primary voice to Deaf KÄnaka Maoli,â€ she said. â€œTo tell our own stories, on our own terms.â€&nbsp; Similar to how Ê»ÅŒlelo HawaiÊ»i was banned in schools in 1896, Nakapueo-Garcia said Indigenous sign languages were also often discouraged or erased as Native communities were pressured to adopt English and other dominant forms of communication such as ASL or learning how to speak or read lips.&nbsp; Therefore, much about how Deaf people communicated in decades past is unknown. Throughout his interviews with Deaf kÅ«puna, Nakapueo-Garcia observed signs he did not recognize.&nbsp;&nbsp; â€œAs they share their stories, Iâ€™m extrapolating different signs that they use. I recognized they are not ASL, and I recognized, from my time with HSL, that those signs are not HSL either,â€ Nakapueo-Garcia said. â€œIâ€™m just trying to gather all the Indigenous signs that are out there, that are spread out across the pae Ê»Äina (archipelago).â€&nbsp; Ê»ÅŒlelo HawaiÊ»i signs are now being developed by Ê»ÅŒlelo HawaiÊ»i Kuhi Lima as other Native communities make similar efforts on the U.S. continent.&nbsp; Melanie McKay-Cody is a deaf assistant professor of Cherokee, Shawnee, Powhatan and Montauk descent who is advising Nakapueo-Garcia. Her initial research revitalizing endangered Indigenous sign languages started with Navajo sign language in 1993 at the University of Arizona. She has since expanded her studies to various tribes working alongside an authentic signer of North American Indian Sign Language.&nbsp; â€œWe are at the point where we are almost complete with a video dictionary that features all the now 14 tribes of American Indians, and there are some differences,â€ she said, through an interpreter.&nbsp; This work is important, she said, because â€œthis is a part of their identity and their heritage.â€ Blending Hawaiian And Deaf Cultures An Ê»ÅŒlelo-based sign language would mean a lot to people like Nikki KepoÊ»o, a Native Hawaiian mom of two in KahaluÊ»u. Her hearing daughter Rebekah Pualokomaikaâ€™i KepoÊ»o, 17, is enrolled at Ke Kula â€˜o Samuel M. Kamakau, a Hawaiian immersion school in KÄneÊ»ohe.&nbsp;Her deaf son, Caleb Laâ€™aikeakua KepoÊ»o, 14, goes to the HawaiÊ»i School for the Deaf and the Blind in Honolulu.&nbsp; KepoÊ»o always planned for her son to enroll in the same Hawaiian immersion program as his sister. When her son was identified as deaf as a baby, she wanted to ensure he was fully immersed in both Native Hawaiian and Deaf culture.&nbsp; But in her opinion, the immersion school isnâ€™t equipped to serve her son with adequate resources and educators steeped in Deaf culture.&nbsp; She expressed the hope for a formal bilingual-bicultural experience within immersion schools â€” a system where both Ê»ÅŒlelo HawaiÊ»i and Native Hawaiian culture, and ASL and Deaf culture, can be integrated.&nbsp; â€œHad there been an option, I would have loved that, because then both my kids would grow up in that same environment, same culture,â€ she said. Unlike immersion schools, HawaiÊ»i School for the Deaf and the Blind has a more American-focused education centered in ASL and American History, according to Nakapueo-Garcia.&nbsp;KepoÊ»o currently sits on the schoolâ€™s community council and has been a strong advocate for providing the Deaf students with cultural opportunities.&nbsp; After he earns his PhD, Nakapueo-Garciaâ€™s goal is to create a program in Hawaiian immersion schools for Deaf keiki and support families like the KepoÊ»o Ê»ohana. He hopes to expand access to immersion education and create a repository of Native signs and have them available for the community to use.&nbsp; â€œWhen they develop a sign language, they can &nbsp; have it in both the deaf school, so deaf kids can learn it as a second language,â€ KepoÊ»o said, â€œand then at Hawaiian immersion, so that hearing kids can also have the option of signing in their Native language.â€ Aunty Keamoai-Kane hopes future generations of deaf children in HawaiÊ»i will receive the education, support and opportunities that were often unavailable to her generation.&nbsp; â€œMy dream is for Deaf people to no longer be left out or left behind,â€ she said. â€œAnd that young and old generations become more comfortable in communicating via â€˜ÅŒlelo Hawaiâ€™i Kuhi Lima.â€&nbsp; The post Deaf And Native Hawaiian: Ê»ÅŒlelo Sign Language Offers A Cultural Connection appeared first on ICT .ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Zu-qIj2QaWiWuexQxiABZMYorsVuqeoaXNlwuX9eHOwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;dUYwj7MLW262u4fAujaV883RZkLKJR1rsysFTP4aFlEƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;sMWsmy-9jZDq4MAYjL0FzeZwxJ05DCO5PTjB-9cyRz4ƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;o0u5mjhpmBcH9WdXY4l_TJxIZBgTh8qjwN889EVD3Fsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;STV1qRzsvrKz8sLamv2f1j70BmAGAyqx-8L7NSrANEAƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkdtrueƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;C9vG0IN4NHOc7_qZIrd1z7S96hESQHX3V4QFPKEgg30ƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ZvIVHPu_AHBsoCohM69ieihEj79bnSLrMGctgnxuRWgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkdTextƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;-8w93xvXIPLTUGMbr6Iarivhr84cjJhjATlgZ25y2-Yƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkqSyndicationSourceƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasef